{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      PostgreSQL + Prisma + NextAuth (Recommended)\n      Full control over the database with type-safe ORM. Complex scheduling logic lives in Next.js API routes with direct DB access.\n      \n        \n          Pros\n          \n            Full SQL power for complex scheduling queries & transactions\n            Type-safe schema with Prisma — catches errors at compile time\n            Easy schema migrations as rules evolve\n            No vendor lock-in — deploy DB anywhere\n            NextAuth handles email/password auth cleanly\n            Complex fairness calculations run server-side with full DB access\n          \n        \n        \n          Cons\n          \n            Need to provision PostgreSQL separately (Neon, Railway, or self-hosted)\n            Slightly more initial setup than Supabase\n            No built-in realtime (not needed for this app)","choice":"prisma","id":null,"timestamp":1776080531666}
{"type":"screen-added","file":"/Users/dimalitvinov/Mac/Dev/Projects/worker/.superpowers/brainstorm/14701-1776080091/content/scheduler-approach.html"}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Weighted Scoring + Greedy Assignment (Recommended)\n      Each candidate guard gets a score for each slot based on fairness history, workload balance, and constraint satisfaction. The highest-scoring eligible guard gets assigned first.\n      \n        Score formula per candidate:\n        + fewer total shifts assigned this period → higher score\n        + fewer night/weekend/opening duties historically → higher score\n        + weekly minimum not yet met → priority boost\n        − conflict risk (same day/next morning) → heavy penalty\n        − over court capacity → excluded entirely\n      \n      \n        \n          Pros\n          \n            Fast — runs in milliseconds even for 150 guards\n            Predictable, explainable results the manager can understand\n            Easy to tune: adjust weights to change fairness priorities\n            Naturally produces warnings when constraints can't be met\n            Works well for 50–150 guards at medium scale\n          \n        \n        \n          Cons\n          \n            Greedy — may not find globally optimal solution\n            Assignment order matters; early slots affect later ones\n            May need multi-pass (2nd pass to fix unmet minimums)","choice":"weighted","id":null,"timestamp":1776080635475}
